+# Birth ownership: slice 1 review contract
+
+Status: reviewed by Astra xhigh with no new P0/P1/P2 findings at `c8faf9bc43a46177dd7f9b21444409b043d22639`; forbidden for production integration.
+
+Baseline: cached `github.com/sagernet/sing-tun@v0.9.6-0.20260924073434-3077c705bbdb`, frozen as source commit `6d4636b`; evidence parent `68b1913cceb196e9706f3dd8c4e863d32ffe0cae`. Design authority: `/home/sway/docs/polaris/design/polaris-construction-disposal-ownership-2026-09-30.md` (Astra xhigh decision recorded on 2026-09-30).
+
+This contract is an implementation review artifact in an isolated dependency worktree. It changes neither the shared module cache nor Polaris dependency manifests, builder replay, or the production build graph. It does not grant a construction disposal capability or an Exact/NoOwner result.
+
+## Decision requiring Astra review
+
+All real autoRedirect backends remain uncovered in slice 1. `Start` must return a recognizable ownership-unsupported error **before** backend initialization, a redirect listener, NFQueue, network-monitor setup, firewall commands, netlink writes, OpenWRT file/reload, Docker reconcile, or Android VPN/bypass/BPF effects. `UpdateRouteAddressSet` must use the same gate before its Android write path. No public flag may bypass this gate.
+
+This deliberately makes autoRedirect unavailable in this isolated intermediate patch. It must not be integrated or published as a production dependency until the applicable backend is covered and the final migration gate is reviewed. Delaying unsupported rejection until after a listener or NFQueue has started would violate the first-effect gate, so the existing real NFQueue fault seam cannot serve as a real-backend Start test in this slice.
+
+Only package-private fake backend injection can drive the slice 1 Start lifecycle in tests. Tests must say that they exercise production lifecycle orchestration with fake resources, not real backend acquisition or live A/B runtime safety. A running old implementation can still delete new resources; slice 1 provides no protection from that old process. A later integration gate must establish absence/termination of legacy writers before admitting a new real backend; a process-local mutex or birth name alone is insufficient.
+
+## Lifecycle
+
+- Construct an immutable random birth token once per `NewAutoRedirect`; entropy failure fails construction. No reuse on retry or restart.
+- Use one mutex plus a condition variable (or equivalent) for lifecycle state, admitted operation count, and callback count. Start is single-use. Duplicate/concurrent Start and Start after Closing are errors.
+- Gate public Start/update and every monitor callback before any writer work. Callback wrapping, rather than `UnregisterCallback` alone, handles callbacks already copied by `monitor_shared.go`.
+- First Close marks Closing while holding only the lifecycle mutex, thereby rejecting new writers/callbacks. It unregisters known callbacks and drains admitted operations/callbacks without holding any lock they require. A registration already Pending may return after Closing: its result still enters the ledger, and a second callback-release pass after drain must unregister that late registration. A one-time early Unregister is insufficient.
+- Start failure leaves its admission permit before invoking rollback/Close. It must not deadlock by waiting for its own permit. Close racing Start observes every registered intent/result and cannot return while the Start operation can still write.
+- Parallel Close callers share one completion result. Completion notification must run even if an acquisition or release panics; panic cannot strand drain counters or concurrent Close waiters. No double resource release. No retry/restart silently reuses this birth.
+- Cleanup calls only ledger-bound release operations. It never calls legacy broad cleanup functions or scans by table prefix, mark, priority, route table, or shared comment.
+
+## Resource ledger
+
+Each entry has immutable birth/domain/exact-resource identity, state, acquisition error, and the bound release operation. Minimum domains include listener, NFQueue, monitor/callback, iptables, nft, netlink, and file/reload. Public caller labels such as `TableName` are not ownership proof.
+
+1. Register intent as Pending before invoking an effect. Duplicate exact keys in one birth are rejected before the effect.
+2. Explicit successful creation/acquisition makes the entry Owned. Observing an existing resource, matching a name, or `EEXIST` never makes it Owned.
+3. A backend-confirmed pre-commit failure releases the intent without deletion. A submitted operation with missing/error ACK, an invalid result, or an interrupted Pending entry becomes sticky Ambiguous. Acquisition/release panic is also sticky Ambiguous, regardless of how much work the backend might have done. Never infer no side effect from an error alone. Slice 1 has no transition that resolves Ambiguous.
+4. Close releases Owned entries in reverse acquisition order through their exact release operations. Each intent records its earlier Owned prerequisites. An unresolved dependent blocks release of its prerequisite: reverse order alone cannot justify releasing a listener/NFQueue/monitor still required by a residual rule/callback. Release error preserves uncertain ownership and returns an error; it cannot mark Released or fall through to broad cleanup.
+5. Ambiguous entries remain unresolved and produce an error/Unknown. Slice 1 does not probe or delete them. Later slices may resolve ambiguity only with a reviewed exact-identity proof.
+6. Released means only that this individual entry was never acquired or its exact release was confirmed. It is not a global disposal result.
+
+## Private operations and fake seam
+
+Introduce only the operations necessary to test these transitions. Keep iptables, nft, netlink, and file/reload operations distinguishable in the ledger and fake. Each operation returns an explicit confirmed-acquire, confirmed-not-committed, or ambiguous result. Default production adapters remain absent in slice 1; future slices install reviewed adapters rather than using the old implementation as a fallback.
+
+There is no existing sing-tun helper combining single-use Start, writer/callback drain, and per-resource ambiguity/dependency states. `redirect_ownership_linux.go` holds this shared lifecycle and ledger; duplicating them in the legacy backend files would produce different ownership decisions. It uses only the standard library and adds no dependency. Callback release captures the original monitor and registration handle; changing a caller's monitor field cannot redirect release to another monitor.
+
+The fake owns an in-memory world keyed by immutable resource identity. It rejects collision/EEXIST, can apply a write and lose its ACK, can fail before a write, and can fail exact release. It records commands/effects so tests can assert that no global deletion or resource belonging to A occurred. No shell executable, real network listener, nft socket, NFQueue, netlink, file/reload, namespace, route, or identity mutation is permitted during these tests.
+
+## Acceptance for this slice
+
+- Unstarted B Close with A's fake resources present causes zero backend operations and preserves A.
+- Real-backend Start/update rejects unsupported before the first possible side effect; both NFT and iptables/Android configurations are covered at the gate.
+- B fails before NFQueue acquisition in the fake lifecycle: zero global deletes and A unchanged. The result does not claim testing real NFQueue.
+- A passes the production Start lifecycle using fake resources. B fails before/after each fake acquisition; confirmed B resources are released exactly once and A stays unchanged.
+- Submitted-write/error-ACK leaves B Ambiguous, never deletes A, and never reports successful exact cleanup.
+- EEXIST/collision never grants ownership or permits deletion of the existing resource.
+- Exact release failure keeps an unresolved state and reports error without global fallback.
+- Acquisition panic, release panic, and invalid ACK remain sticky Ambiguous. Parallel Close callers receive the same final result, including these errors, and none remains blocked on a missing completion notification.
+- Close waits for latched Start/update/callback work; callbacks already snapshotted by a monitor reject entry after Closing; no wait holds a callback-required lock.
+- An already-Pending callback registration returns during Close drain, is ledgered, and is finally unregistered. An unresolved dependent retains its required resources while independent resources still release.
+- Parallel Close releases each confirmed resource at most once; Start after Close and repeated Start fail without writes.
+- Run targeted lifecycle tests with Go 1.25.5, cached alternate modfile, network disabled (`GOPROXY=off GOSUMDB=off`), and `-race`. Run build/type checks for relevant Linux/Android source only as supported by the cached dependency graph. Leave original go.mod/go.sum and evidence modfiles unchanged.
+
+## Separate later review gates
+
+Slice 2 covers exact identities and per-resource iptables/nft/netlink acquire/delete, removes startup stale cleanup, and proves IPv4/IPv6/netns behavior in fakes. Slice 3 covers OpenWRT file/reload separately from NFT commit, owner-specific Docker reconcile, Android VPN/bypass/BPF borrowed state, and genuinely shared-resource claims. Slice 4 reviews the pinned patch manifest, replay hashes, Polaris dependency graph, legacy/new coexistence gate, Linux/Android consumer builds, and isolated namespace/device verification. Each slice stops for Astra review before advancing.
+
+The Polaris integration proposal is a separate change: generate the production source delta against frozen source baseline `6d4636b`, identify evidence-only modfiles separately, pin the complete dependency patch and replay hashes, and verify the builder's resolved sing-tun module/source identity. The production dependency/build graph and patch manifest require Astra review before merge. Do not replace the dependency with this intermediate worktree. Establish a cross-process/namespace gate proving legacy writers have exited before enabling any real backend; then build actual Linux/Android consumers and verify them in the separately authorized namespace/device window. Missing backend coverage, missing legacy-exit evidence, or ambiguous cleanup must keep runtime P1 Unknown.
+
+## Isolated validation on 2026-09-30
+
+All commands below passed. The first executes only ownership fakes with the race detector; vet performs static checking; the Android command compiles the package and test sources for arm64 but uses `/bin/true` as the executor, so it does **not** execute an Android binary or claim Android device/runtime validation. No original or alternate go.mod/go.sum file was changed.
+
+```sh
+cd /home/sway/Code/sing-tun-birth-owner-sol-20260930
+env GOWORK=off GOTOOLCHAIN=local GOPROXY=off GOSUMDB=off \
+  /home/sway/go/pkg/mod/golang.org/toolchain@v0.0.1-go1.25.5.linux-amd64/bin/go \
+  test -mod=readonly -modfile=ownership-evidence.mod -race -count=1 \
+  -run 'TestRedirectOwnership|TestOwnershipEvidence' .
+env GOWORK=off GOTOOLCHAIN=local GOPROXY=off GOSUMDB=off \
+  /home/sway/go/pkg/mod/golang.org/toolchain@v0.0.1-go1.25.5.linux-amd64/bin/go \
+  vet -mod=readonly -modfile=ownership-evidence.mod .
+env GOWORK=off GOTOOLCHAIN=local GOPROXY=off GOSUMDB=off \
+  CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=android GOARCH=arm64 \
+  /home/sway/go/pkg/mod/golang.org/toolchain@v0.0.1-go1.25.5.linux-amd64/bin/go \
+  test -mod=readonly -modfile=ownership-evidence.mod -run '^$' -exec /bin/true .
+```
+
+The fake matrix covers running A preserved across every B acquisition stage, confirmed rollback, lost ACK, collision/EEXIST, invalid ACK, acquire/release panic, residual prerequisites, duplicate/foreign intents, concurrent Close result identity, Start/update drain, both monitor types' callback snapshots and late registrations, callback/backend panic completion, and exact release on a shared monitor. Existing unrelated integration tests requiring real networking were not run.
