+# Slice 2a: exact iptables identities, fake-only
+
+Parent: Astra-reviewed Slice 1 `c8faf9bc43a46177dd7f9b21444409b043d22639`. The isolated fake-only Slice 2a and its `/0` correction `e3de453d56a2b3fd2c95d49cd46ebe7f85e26097` have completed independent Astra review with no remaining P0/P1/P2 finding. **No production integration is authorized.** Every real backend still returns `ErrAutoRedirectOwnershipUnsupported` before its first side effect; there is no real iptables adapter or legacy setup/cleanup fallback. No Exact/NoOwner capability or runtime P1 conclusion is introduced.
+
+The correction after `1bdc5c0caff57b5412f01a06b1465bdb6974f770` removes the raw-argv blacklist. Astra found that xtables abbreviations and combined short options could change the physical table or executor despite that blacklist, so the original slice's argv-to-identity contract was insufficient. This revision replaces it with a closed grammar and typed generation; it does not add more blacklist exceptions.
+
+Astra's review of `63105a07c2f55208e35adef7e166b879ef3ee270` closed the parser finding and identified one remaining native-identity alias: omitted source/destination matches and explicit family-wide `/0` prefixes. The common argv generator now omits every valid prefix with Bits()==0 for both private rules and shared-hook references. It preserves `0.0.0.0/32` and `::/128`; an unspecified address alone is not a wildcard.
+
+## Source scope
+
+`redirect_ownership_iptables_linux.go` adds typed physical identities for namespace device/inode, IPv4/IPv6, table, chain, and complete rule argv. Future OS adapters must resolve and retain the actual namespace handle; this fake-only slice neither resolves a namespace pathname nor enters a namespace. A path alias or a reused inode without a retained handle cannot establish ownership.
+
+Private chain names derive from birth plus namespace/family/table/role, use 128 hash bits and 28 ASCII bytes, and do not use the public `TableName` as a cleanup prefix. Chain acquisition remains conditional on the explicit create ACK. A name collision/EEXIST never grants ownership. Rules and references include a full birth comment and an exact immutable delete-by-spec identity. No caller argv is submitted: typed values generate the canonical recorded argv, and every argv passed to a backend is cloned so mutation cannot change the recorded delete target.
+
+The four private operations are CreateChain, AddRule, DeleteRule, and DeleteChain. They expose no scan, flush, shared policy mutation, or prefix deletion. Every create/add goes through Slice 1 Pending-before-effect and ACK classification. Each private-chain rule and each shared-hook reference records the private chain as an Owned prerequisite. Reverse release deletes exact rules first; an ambiguous acquisition or failed reference deletion keeps its required chain rather than flushing it. Shared builtin chains are never acquired or deleted.
+
+The complete input grammar accepts each of `-p`, `-s`, `-d`, `--sport`, `--dport` at most once as a separate flag/value pair. Private rules also require exactly one `-j` paired with ACCEPT, DROP, or RETURN; shared-hook references cannot supply a jump and use only their Owned private chain target. Protocol input is limited to tcp/6 or udp/17 and becomes numeric 6/17. Addresses must be numeric, match the explicit IPv4/IPv6 scope, and become masked prefixes. Ports must be decimal uint16 values and become canonical decimal; ports require a protocol. The generator emits a fixed order, explicitly selects the tcp/udp match module when ports are present, and appends its generated birth comment and target.
+
+All other tokens are rejected before a rule commit, including abbreviated/equals options, combined or attached short flags, family selection, executor/modprobe/wait controls, user match modules/comments, DNS names, service names, ranges, multiple addresses, and unknown targets. Unsupported namespace/family/table, unsupported table/hook combinations, duplicate flags, NUL/invalid UTF-8, mapped IPv4-in-IPv6, and zone-qualified addresses are also rejected. This deliberately narrow grammar covers selected test fixtures and hooks; it does not establish coverage of the complete existing firewall builder or packet semantics.
+
+`redirect_nftables.go` now directly returns the callback registration result instead of discarding it. The legacy nft setup remains unreachable from the real public dispatcher and was not run. NFT ownership/commit boundaries and netlink route/table/priority identities are the next separate Slice 2 pieces.
+
+No existing helper provides typed exact resource identities: the old iptables builder uses fixed-name chains and broad cleanup. The new helper reuses Slice 1 lifecycle/ledger and existing table constants, uses only the standard library, and does not invoke the legacy builder or cleanup methods.
+
+## Fake verification
+
+The fake oracle independently stores full physical chain tuples and rule argv, preserves an existing legacy chain/reference and running A, and refuses foreign deletion. The matrix covers two namespace identities, IPv4/IPv6, nat/mangle/filter, and each chain/private-rule/reference commit with confirmed pre-commit failure, missing ACK, post-commit panic, and invalid ACK. Additional tests cover:
+
+- Same generated chain name in another namespace/family surviving exact release.
+- A/B with the same caller table label, private chain name collision/EEXIST, and duplicate namespace-alias intents.
+- Failed/panicking reference release retaining its chain; chain release failure staying Ambiguous; repeated Close not retrying unknown deletion.
+- Caller/backend argv mutation, invalid scope/hook/flags, and foreign chain references rejected before rule commit.
+- A submitted reference latched across Closing, with Close draining before exact release.
+- The reported `--tabl=mangle`, `-vtmangle`, `--comm`, `-4`/`-6`, and `-M`/`--modprobe` counterexamples, plus full/attached control forms and unknown flags, rejected by the closed grammar before AddRule.
+- Numeric IPv4/IPv6 positive fixtures, ACCEPT/DROP/RETURN targets, source/destination/ports, reordered flags, protocol aliases, and leading-zero ports generating the expected canonical argv. Equivalent inputs produce one physical ledger identity; reference targets come only from the recorded Owned chain.
+- A 48-case matrix of IPv4/IPv6, source/destination/both, private/reference, omitted-first/explicit-first, and `/0`/host prefixes. Same-birth/chain/role `/0` aliases return the same resource identity and the second attempt is refused before AddRule; `/32` and `/128` remain two distinct resources. Exact release argv and legacy state preservation are checked in both cases.
+
+The fake treats duplicate rule insertion as confirmed no creation. This is an oracle for the ledger contract, **not** a claim about a real iptables command's duplicate/EEXIST behavior. Real adapter ACK proof, command locking, duplicate-spec/external-writer behavior, namespace handle lifetime, NFQueue/listener/route prerequisites, and actual firewall packet behavior require later reviewed adapters and the separately authorized namespace/device window.
+
+## Passed checks
+
+Final commands passed with cached Go 1.25.5 and unchanged original/alternate module files. Race runs only ownership fakes; vet is static; Android arm64 compilation uses `/bin/true` as the executor and does not run an Android binary. No real network, firewall, NFQueue, netlink, route, namespace, or identity mutation was executed.
+
+Post-correction receipt: Linux race `ok github.com/sagernet/sing-tun 1.092s`; Linux vet exit 0; Android arm64 compile-only `ok github.com/sagernet/sing-tun 0.001s`. Module proxy downloads and checksum database requests were disabled for every command below; the grammar performs no DNS or service-name lookup.
+
+The subsequent `/0` correction passed all iptables fake tests under race: `ok github.com/sagernet/sing-tun 1.089s`, using the same cached/offline module settings and `-run '^TestRedirectOwnershipIPTables'`. Only the common prefix emission condition, its tests, and this receipt changed from `63105a0`; the prior vet and Android compile receipts belong to that reviewed baseline and were not rerun for this small correction.
+
+```sh
+cd /home/sway/Code/sing-tun-birth-owner-sol-20260930
+env GOWORK=off GOTOOLCHAIN=local GOPROXY=off GOSUMDB=off \
+  /home/sway/go/pkg/mod/golang.org/toolchain@v0.0.1-go1.25.5.linux-amd64/bin/go \
+  test -mod=readonly -modfile=ownership-evidence.mod -race -count=1 \
+  -run 'TestRedirectOwnership|TestOwnershipEvidence' .
+env GOWORK=off GOTOOLCHAIN=local GOPROXY=off GOSUMDB=off \
+  /home/sway/go/pkg/mod/golang.org/toolchain@v0.0.1-go1.25.5.linux-amd64/bin/go \
+  vet -mod=readonly -modfile=ownership-evidence.mod .
+env GOWORK=off GOTOOLCHAIN=local GOPROXY=off GOSUMDB=off \
+  CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=android GOARCH=arm64 \
+  /home/sway/go/pkg/mod/golang.org/toolchain@v0.0.1-go1.25.5.linux-amd64/bin/go \
+  test -mod=readonly -modfile=ownership-evidence.mod -run '^$' -exec /bin/true .
+```
+
+The public module cache, Polaris dependency/build graph, patch manifest, and pinned dependency remain unchanged. Retained namespace handles, fixed legacy-versus-nft executables and executor settings, actual duplicate-spec/ACK behavior, and command locking remain requirements for a future real adapter. OpenWRT/Docker/Android VPN/bypass/BPF, NFT, netlink, legacy-writer exit, and production consumer validation remain gated. Freeze this corrected small source slice for Astra before proceeding to the next backend.
